\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Notation}
\title{Notation for Clifford algebras}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The anticommutator is the scalar #{uv+vu=B_q(u,v)}
(\ref{fdip-000P}, \ref{fdip-00R2}).}

\p{A vector acts as #{v\wedge+v\cdot}
(\ref{fdip-00O5}, \ref{fdip-00R3}).}

\p{The derivative is #{\cov=\covdot+\covwedge}, with no sign between
the parts. Under a positive inner product, #{\extdag=-\covdot}.}

\p{Reversion is #{\tilde A}. The pseudoscalar is #{\psI}
(\ref{fdip-00O5}, \ref{fdip-00R3}).}

\p{The centered grid operator #{D} is not the Kähler–Dirac operator
#{\extD} (\ref{fdip-000R}, \ref{fdip-00R6}).}

\p{The factor #{\ui} in the Fourier symbol #{D(p)} is the eigenvalue
of the centered difference (\ref{fdip-000S}, \ref{fdip-00R4}).}

\p{A vector acts on the exterior algebra as an outer part plus an
inner part, #{v\wedge+v\cdot}, and the derivative splits the same
way, #{\cov=\covdot+\covwedge}. Writing both with one product shows
that the Clifford action and the derivative are one construction,
and that the adjoint #{\extdag=-\covdot} differs from the inner part
only by the sign a positive inner product introduces. Generators are
elements of the algebra, not matrices, so a relation such as
#{uv+vu=B_q(u,v)} holds without a choice of representation.
Reversion #{\tilde A} and the pseudoscalar #{\psI} stay distinct
from the adjoint #{A^{\dagger}} and the identity #{\bbI}.}
